{
  "summary": "This pull request moves block writing, index updates, and pruning file bookkeeping from validation's FlushStateToDisk into BlockManager. Validation continues to calculate allowable pruning height limits and sizes, while BlockManager manages prune locks, file selection, flushing, and file deletion.",
  "problem": "Block storage flushing and pruning logic is tightly coupled with validation in FlushStateToDisk, which directly manipulates block directory space, prune locks, and file deletion. This prevents block storage and pruning from operating as a clean standalone interface, hindering libbitcoinkernel modularization efforts.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: 'A pruning API on block storage (#35627) would have to go through validation for all of it. This moves the storage side into BlockManager, as described in the same issue.'",
    "Commit fb911aecde: moves block and undo file flushing, block index writes, and pruned file deletion out of FlushStateToDisk into BlockManager."
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [
      "#36735"
    ],
    "enables": [
      "#35627"
    ]
  },
  "scope_notes": "This PR is stacked on PR #36735; the first four commits address failed flush retries and are inherited from that base PR.",
  "changed_since_previous": "",
  "card": "This pull request refactors block storage writing and pruning out of validation's FlushStateToDisk and into BlockManager. Validation passes prune limits while BlockManager handles file selection, prune locks, index writes, and file deletions. This separation untangles validation from storage internals to enable a clean pruning API for the kernel project as tracked in issue #35627. The PR is stacked on and depends on #36735.",
